import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BOeh4kYD.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-12","description":"2026-09-12 dual-source monitoring of core Agent researchers, runtime projects, and the CodeFlowMu competitor radar.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-12","description":"2026-09-12 dual-source monitoring of core Agent researchers, runtime projects, and the CodeFlowMu competitor radar.","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-12.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-12.md","lastUpdated":1789173712000}'),n={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-12.md"};function i(s,e,c,l,d,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-09-12" tabindex="-1">Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-12 <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-09-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-12&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Asia/Shanghai · Only meaningful technical or product developments are included.</p></blockquote><p><a href="./">Back to the daily archive</a></p><h2 id="daily-conclusion" tabindex="-1">Daily conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#daily-conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Daily conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two lines are accelerating at the same time: <strong>Project / Channel / Mobile are becoming formal control surfaces for long-running agents</strong>, while <strong>historical sessions, retained providers, old sandbox state, and previous approvals no longer imply current execution authority and must be re-proven near the actual effect boundary.</strong></p><h2 id="_1-cursor-projects-coordinator-massive-subagent-fan-out-month-scale-context-subscriptions-enter-beta" tabindex="-1">1. Cursor Projects: coordinator + massive subagent fan-out + month-scale context + subscriptions enter Beta <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-cursor-projects-coordinator-massive-subagent-fan-out-month-scale-context-subscriptions-enter-beta" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Cursor Projects: coordinator + massive subagent fan-out + month-scale context + subscriptions enter Beta&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: BETA / OFFICIAL RELEASE (officially published 2026-09-10; added to the archive in this run)</strong></p><p>Cursor officially launched <a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/projects" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Projects</a>. A Project is designed for larger bodies of work such as features, migrations, and full applications. It preserves context over months and uses a coordinator agent that plans and delegates rather than writing code itself. The coordinator creates and manages implementation agents and can scale parallel work to large fleets of subagents.</p><p>Projects run on dedicated cloud computers, so work continues after the user&#39;s laptop closes. When local-machine testing is required, the coordinator can start a local agent. Project context is stored in files that sync across cloud and local machines, and Projects can subscribe to Slack, schedules, and PR activity so recurring or event-driven work can happen without a new prompt.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this is not merely another coding-agent feature. It combines a durable work object, a coordinator, parallel workers, multi-host execution, shared context, and recurring/event-driven triggers into one formal product surface.</p><p><strong>CodeFlowMu/FCoP relevance:</strong> this strongly overlaps with CodeFlowMu&#39;s PM + multi-role/multi-agent + long-running work + cloud/local Host direction. CodeFlowMu should continue differentiating through responsibility roles, authorization boundaries, independent EVAL, recoverable facts, and FCoP protocol evidence.</p><p><strong>Learning: very high | Competitive risk: very high | Radar: treat Cursor Projects as a top-priority external reference.</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction value: very high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>When hundreds or thousands of subagents operate across cloud and local machines, how are shared-context authorship, conflict convergence, permission inheritance, and compaction provenance proven?</em></p><h2 id="_2-yue-zhao-anywhere-agents-v0-8-0-different-models-take-different-jobs-followed-by-a-windows-sandbox-reality-check" tabindex="-1">2. Yue Zhao / anywhere-agents v0.8.0: different models take different jobs, followed by a Windows sandbox reality check <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-yue-zhao-anywhere-agents-v0-8-0-different-models-take-different-jobs-followed-by-a-windows-sandbox-reality-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Yue Zhao / anywhere-agents v0.8.0: different models take different jobs, followed by a Windows sandbox reality check&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: RELEASED v0.8.0 + POST-RELEASE FIX</strong></p><p>Yue Zhao released <a href="https://github.com/yzhao062/anywhere-agents/releases/tag/v0.8.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">anywhere-agents v0.8.0</a>. The most relevant change is explicit model-role allocation: <code>prun</code> gives a larger share of parallel units to Agy/Sonnet, while Codex is reserved for the <code>/vet</code> gatekeeper role, partly because its current credit cost is a poor fit for bulk fan-out. Agy units support scratch or throwaway-clone execution, follow-up turns with <code>--continue-from</code>, and quota monitoring.</p><p>Zhao then committed a Windows-specific correction. <code>dispatch-task-agy.py</code> had passed <code>--sandbox</code> to every unit; on Windows, Agy&#39;s exebox relaunched an elevated broker, producing a UAC prompt for each shell-using unit. The dispatcher now omits that sandbox by default and makes it explicit opt-in through <code>PRUN_AGY_SANDBOX</code>. The documentation deliberately describes the remaining boundary as <strong>structural containment, not enforced isolation</strong>; a live probe without the flag successfully used shell, web fetch, and file writes.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> it shows two practical engineering decisions at once. Model differences can be handled by job/cost/capability routing rather than pretending all models are interchangeable. At the same time, a sandbox claim that is operationally unusable on the real Host must be downgraded to an explicit isolation-strength fact rather than silently treated as strong isolation.</p><p><strong>CodeFlowMu/FCoP relevance:</strong> PM/DEV/QA/EVAL model selection can be made explicit around role capability, cost, and independence. Host capability records should distinguish <code>structural containment</code> from a genuinely <code>enforced sandbox</code>.</p><p><strong>Interaction value: very high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Would you record both role/model routing and current isolation strength in an auditable execution receipt so <code>/vet</code> can judge the environment under which a result was produced?</em></p><h2 id="_3-openai-codex-a-long-lived-thread-no-longer-owns-static-provider-host-sandbox-authority" tabindex="-1">3. OpenAI Codex: a long-lived Thread no longer owns static Provider / Host / Sandbox authority <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-openai-codex-a-long-lived-thread-no-longer-owns-static-provider-host-sandbox-authority" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. OpenAI Codex: a long-lived Thread no longer owns static Provider / Host / Sandbox authority&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: MERGED / MERGED / MERGED</strong></p><p>Three related Codex changes landed together:</p><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/44944" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#44944</a>: existing app-server threads re-check current managed <code>model_provider</code> / <code>model_providers</code> requirements before turn start/steer, review, compaction, manual queue start, and active-goal updates. If requirements cannot be loaded or the retained provider no longer matches, the operation is rejected and the user is prompted to restart.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/44939" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#44939</a>: Windows sandbox setup now respects the actual execution host. A local TUI cannot configure a remote executor&#39;s sandbox; remote servers own their Agent permission selection, and mixed local/remote executors are surfaced explicitly.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/44945" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#44945</a>: Windows sandbox setup is routed through the app server and the effective sandbox mode must be verified before Agent mode is enabled. If a setup request times out or its response is lost, setup remains pending and input stays locked rather than assuming success; interrupted setup gets explicit restart semantics after reconnect.</li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> <code>the thread still exists</code>, <code>this provider was used last time</code>, and <code>the UI started setup</code> are no longer accepted as evidence of current authority. Provider, execution host, and sandbox readiness are live admission facts.</p><p><strong>CodeFlowMu/FCoP relevance:</strong> Host admission and recovery should bind <strong>Thread/Run Identity + Provider Identity + Execution Host Identity + Effective Sandbox Capability + Policy Revision</strong>, rather than merely restoring a session ID.</p><p><strong>Interaction value: very high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Will these checks converge into one executor capability/authority digest that is revalidated before every resume, turn, and approval?</em></p><h2 id="_4-paperclip-imessage-is-being-developed-as-a-durable-remote-command-and-interaction-channel-for-digital-employees" tabindex="-1">4. Paperclip: iMessage is being developed as a durable remote command and interaction channel for Digital Employees <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-paperclip-imessage-is-being-developed-as-a-durable-remote-command-and-interaction-channel-for-digital-employees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Paperclip: iMessage is being developed as a durable remote command and interaction channel for Digital Employees&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: DRAFT / EXPERIMENTAL / live qualification incomplete</strong></p><p>Draft PR <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/13299" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#13299</a> adds experimental <strong>iMessage Photon</strong>. The goal is a stable Photon Cloud phone number per agent channel so a person can start a task, send a photo, or answer an agent&#39;s pending question from Apple Messages. DMs and explicitly enabled groups reuse the existing channel admission, identity links, task generations, publication, and interaction-continuation infrastructure.</p><p>This is more than a chat adapter. The design persists immutable text/file send identities, upload receipts, poll and option IDs, per-person drafts, and canonical interaction-continuation proofs; recovery uses a leased receiver plus checkpoints, while media handling covers HEIC/HEIF, JPEG previews, and Live Photo companion video retention.</p><p>The qualification boundary is explicit: there are no live Photon credentials/test participants yet, and real iPhone DM/group, media, poll/text continuation, restart, and ambiguous-send tests remain unrun. The PR therefore remains Draft and experimental.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> remote Digital Employee control is moving beyond Web/PWA into normal messaging surfaces, and the correct architecture is not a simple chatbot—it preserves sender identity, task generation, continuation, and delivery facts.</p><p><strong>Learning: very high | Competitive risk: high | Radar: keep Paperclip at the highest priority and add IM remote-control channels as a dedicated dimension.</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction value: very high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Will iMessage approvals/answers produce typed human-action receipts bound to sender identity, task generation, and the exact question/effect digest instead of merely feeding message text back to the agent?</em></p><h2 id="_5-paperclip-a-call-time-authorization-hardening-attempt-was-deliberately-closed-exposing-the-compatibility-boundary-of-global-fail-closed-enforcement" tabindex="-1">5. Paperclip: a call-time authorization hardening attempt was deliberately closed, exposing the compatibility boundary of global fail-closed enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-paperclip-a-call-time-authorization-hardening-attempt-was-deliberately-closed-exposing-the-compatibility-boundary-of-global-fail-closed-enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Paperclip: a call-time authorization hardening attempt was deliberately closed, exposing the compatibility boundary of global fail-closed enforcement&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: CLOSED / NOT MERGED / NOT DEPLOYED</strong></p><p>PR <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/13297" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#13297</a> attempted to move final authorization for approval-governed remote HTTP MCP actions to the point after all awaited preparation and immediately before the first provider operation. It used PostgreSQL time for the expiry boundary, atomically consumed request/invocation/audit disposition, and blocked credential-refresh + redispatch after an approved provider call returned HTTP 401. Its intended safety properties included no <code>await</code> between successful permit and HTTP write, at-most-one provider operation per approval, and <code>uncertain</code> rather than replay when the response outcome is lost.</p><p>The author closed the PR without merge or deployment. The focused 71-test PostgreSQL proof passed, but the provider-wide restrictions disabled or conflicted with existing legacy, Test-tab, local-stdio/built-in, and explicit-retry ask-first paths. Upstream CI had 12 server failures and all three E2E shards conflicted with the reduced contract, so the bounded rollout stop condition was used.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this is a valuable failure sample. Call-time authorization cannot simply be bolted in front of every provider with one global rejection rule. Each transport/execution path needs explicit single-effect, retry, statefulness, and approval semantics.</p><p><strong>CodeFlowMu/FCoP relevance:</strong> Host/Tool authorization is better modeled as a <strong>per-effect-path contract</strong>: which paths can guarantee single dispatch, which must fail closed, and which require compensation.</p><p><strong>Learning: very high | Competitive risk: high (architecture-complexity risk) | Radar: do not downgrade Paperclip; this is evidence that it is colliding with real production authorization boundaries.</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction value: high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Will the next version implement final permit per transport/provider capability rather than globally denying older paths that do not satisfy the single-dispatch contract?</em></p><h2 id="_6-superset-agent-fleet-status-formally-reaches-the-iphone-lock-screen-and-dynamic-island" tabindex="-1">6. Superset: Agent fleet status formally reaches the iPhone Lock Screen and Dynamic Island <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-superset-agent-fleet-status-formally-reaches-the-iphone-lock-screen-and-dynamic-island" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Superset: Agent fleet status formally reaches the iPhone Lock Screen and Dynamic Island&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: MERGED (requires a native build/TestFlight; not an OTA-only change)</strong></p><p>PR <a href="https://github.com/superset-sh/superset/pull/7452" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#7452</a> restores and merges the iOS <code>AgentActivity</code> Live Activity. The Lock Screen can show up to four agent rows; Dynamic Island shows agent count and elapsed time; rows support deep links and localization.</p><p>The important product signal is not the card styling: <strong>agent-fleet state is now an operating-system-level mobile surface</strong>. The current limitations are equally important. This is a native extension that must ship in a subsequent TestFlight/preview build, and updates are currently foreground-oriented; after the app closes, the card becomes stale and Dynamic Island freezes at the last snapshot. It is therefore not yet a fully push-driven remote supervisor.</p><p><strong>Learning: very high | Competitive risk: high | Radar: Superset is very close to the primary radar; increase the mobile-control weight again.</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction value: high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Will the next step use server push / ActivityKit remote updates to deliver typed review, permission, and failure state continuously to the Lock Screen and bind approvals to receipts?</em></p><h2 id="_7-orca-dst-fall-back-can-relaunch-one-automation-every-scheduler-tick-exposing-the-need-for-durable-occurrence-identity" tabindex="-1">7. Orca: DST fall-back can relaunch one Automation every scheduler tick, exposing the need for durable occurrence identity <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-orca-dst-fall-back-can-relaunch-one-automation-every-scheduler-tick-exposing-the-need-for-durable-occurrence-identity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Orca: DST fall-back can relaunch one Automation every scheduler tick, exposing the need for durable occurrence identity&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Status: OPEN</strong></p><p>PR <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/20179" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#20179</a> fixes a scheduler failure in which a cron fire time inside the repeated autumn DST hour could resolve the “next occurrence” back to the same instant that just fired. The scheduler then saw <code>nextRunAt &lt;= now</code> on every roughly 60-second tick and launched the agent again. In <code>new_per_run</code> mode, every duplicate launch also created a new worktree and consumed additional tokens.</p><p>The root cause was local wall-clock field recomposition in <code>floorToMinute</code>: an ambiguous minute could resolve to the earlier offset and violate the “strictly after” contract. The fix switches flooring to UTC arithmetic and adds an actual <code>America/New_York 2026</code> fall-back regression: both legitimate repeated-hour occurrences fire once, while the nonexistent spring-forward minute is still skipped.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> scheduler arithmetic errors can materialize directly as duplicate agents and worktrees, and can become duplicate external side effects if there is no independent occurrence identity.</p><p><strong>CodeFlowMu/FCoP relevance:</strong> each scheduled occurrence should have a durable idempotency identity such as <code>(schedule_id, intended_occurrence_instant)</code>. A scheduler regression may produce duplicate wake signals, but it should not be able to create a second business effect.</p><p><strong>Learning: very high | Competitive risk: medium-high | Radar: raise Orca&#39;s Automation Runtime weight further.</strong></p><p><strong>Interaction value: very high.</strong> Suggested question: <em>Would you persist every scheduled occurrence as a global idempotency key so DST/clock/restart errors can at worst duplicate a wake, not duplicate the business run?</em></p><h2 id="cross-project-convergence" tabindex="-1">Cross-project convergence <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-convergence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project convergence&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A clearer split is emerging:</p><blockquote><p><strong>Control surfaces can become richer, but authority must become narrower and move closer to the real effect.</strong></p></blockquote><p>Cursor Projects, Paperclip iMessage, and Superset Lock Screen expand the places from which long-running agents can be coordinated. At the same time, Codex separates Provider/Host/Sandbox authority from historical Thread state; Paperclip&#39;s failed rollout shows that final authorization must understand the concrete transport&#39;s effect contract; and Orca shows that even a Schedule needs an independent occurrence identity.</p><p>A useful CodeFlowMu/FCoP chain is:</p><p><strong>Control Surface / Trigger → Human or System Principal → Work/Run Identity → Live Host &amp; Capability Evidence → Call-time Authority → Effect Identity → Settlement / Replay Eligibility</strong>.</p><p>Yue Zhao&#39;s v0.8.0 adds a useful organizational conclusion: <strong>model differences do not have to be erased. Models can be treated as role resources, with fan-out vs gatekeeper duties chosen by capability, cost, and independence.</strong></p><h2 id="dual-source-note" tabindex="-1">Dual-source note <a class="header-anchor" href="#dual-source-note" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dual-source note&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This run checked first-party GitHub PRs/releases, the official Cursor Changelog/Blog, and fresh public search results. No reliably attributable social post from the monitored people added more technical value than the first-party engineering sources above, so marketing reposts and uncertain identities were not used to pad the report.</p><p>No new commits were found in Yue Zhao&#39;s <code>grade</code>, <code>auditable</code>, or <code>agent-audit</code>; the Yue Zhao update reported here comes from his maintained <code>anywhere-agents</code> / <code>agent-config</code> v0.8.0 work and the subsequent Windows fix.</p>',67)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
